WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:03.670
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:03.670 --> 00:00:11.349
You’re in for a real treat today, because
today we’re not going to simulate just plain

00:00:11.349 --> 00:00:12.639
regular fluids.

00:00:12.639 --> 00:00:13.639
No!

00:00:13.639 --> 00:00:16.139
We’re going to simulate ferrofluids!

00:00:16.139 --> 00:00:21.610
These are fluids that have magnetic properties
and respond to an external magnetic field

00:00:21.610 --> 00:00:26.070
and you will see in a moment that they are
able to even climb things.

00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:30.860
You see in the reference footage here that
this also means if there is no magnetic field,

00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:33.470
we have a regular fluid simulation.

00:00:33.470 --> 00:00:35.710
Nothing too crazy here.

00:00:35.710 --> 00:00:40.750
In this real-world footage, we have a tray
of ferrofluid up in the air, and we have a

00:00:40.750 --> 00:00:48.780
magnet below it, so as the tray descends down
and gets closer to the magnet, this happens.

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:53.149
But the strength of the magnetic field is
not the only factor that a simulation needs

00:00:53.149 --> 00:00:54.350
to take into account.

00:00:54.350 --> 00:00:59.860
Here’s another real experiment that shows
that the orientation of the magnet also makes

00:00:59.860 --> 00:01:05.269
a great deal of difference to the distortions
of the fluid surface.

00:01:05.269 --> 00:01:07.880
And now, let’s have a look at some simulations!

00:01:07.880 --> 00:01:13.969
This simulation reproduces the rotating magnet
experiment that you’ve seen a second ago.

00:01:13.969 --> 00:01:20.109
It works great, what is even more, if we are
in a simulation, we can finally do things

00:01:20.109 --> 00:01:26.490
that would be either expensive, or impossible
in the real life, so let’s do exactly that!

00:01:26.490 --> 00:01:36.670
You see a steel sphere attracting the ferrofluid
here, and, now, the strength of the magnet

00:01:36.670 --> 00:01:42.350
within is decreased, giving us the impression
that we can bend this fluid to our will!

00:01:42.350 --> 00:01:44.399
How cool is that?

00:01:44.399 --> 00:01:50.609
In the simulation, we can also experiment
with arbitrarily-shaped magnets.

00:01:50.609 --> 00:01:56.090
And here is the legendary real experiment
where with magnetism, we can make a ferrofluid

00:01:56.090 --> 00:01:59.369
climb up on this steel helix.

00:01:59.369 --> 00:02:00.369
Look at that.

00:02:00.369 --> 00:02:04.939
When I’ve first seen this video and started
reading the paper, I was just giggling like

00:02:04.939 --> 00:02:06.280
a little girl.

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:08.099
So good.

00:02:08.099 --> 00:02:12.829
Just imagine how hard it is to do something
where have footage from the real world that

00:02:12.829 --> 00:02:18.489
keeps judging our simulation results, and
we are only done when there is a near-exact

00:02:18.489 --> 00:02:20.430
match, such as the one you see here.

00:02:20.430 --> 00:02:22.799
Huge congratulations to the authors.

00:02:22.799 --> 00:02:27.510
You see here how the simulation output depends
on the number of iterations.

00:02:27.510 --> 00:02:33.069
More iterations means that we redo the calculations
over and over again, and get results closer

00:02:33.069 --> 00:02:38.040
to what would happen in real life at the cost
of more computation time.

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:43.769
However, as you see, we can get close to the
real solution with even 1 iteration, which

00:02:43.769 --> 00:02:45.250
is remarkable.

00:02:45.250 --> 00:02:49.689
In my own fluid simulation experiments, when
I tried to solve the pressure field, using

00:02:49.689 --> 00:02:55.510
1 to 4 iterations gave me a result that’s
not only inaccurate, but singular, which blows

00:02:55.510 --> 00:02:58.040
up the simulation.

00:02:58.040 --> 00:02:59.090
Look at this.

00:02:59.090 --> 00:03:04.769
On this axis, you can see how the fluid disturbances
get more pronounced as a response to a stronger

00:03:04.769 --> 00:03:06.239
magnetic field.

00:03:06.239 --> 00:03:12.330
And in this direction, you see how the effect
of surface tension smooths out these shapes.

00:03:12.330 --> 00:03:14.090
What a visualization!

00:03:14.090 --> 00:03:19.109
The information density in this example is
just out of this world… and it is still

00:03:19.109 --> 00:03:22.020
both informative, and beautiful.

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:26.571
If only I could tell you how many times I
have to remake each of the figures in my papers

00:03:26.571 --> 00:03:31.370
in pursuit of this…I can only imagine how
long it took to finish this one.

00:03:31.370 --> 00:03:32.370
Bravo.

00:03:32.370 --> 00:03:36.209
And if all that’s not enough for you to
fall out of your chair, get this.

00:03:36.209 --> 00:03:39.870
It is about Libo Huang, the first author of
this paper.

00:03:39.870 --> 00:03:46.099
I became quite curious about his other works,
and have found exactly zero of them.

00:03:46.099 --> 00:03:48.310
This was his first paper.

00:03:48.310 --> 00:03:49.650
My goodness.

00:03:49.650 --> 00:03:55.159
And of course, it takes a team to create such
a work, so congratulations to all three authors,

00:03:55.159 --> 00:03:57.409
this is one heck of a paper.

00:03:57.409 --> 00:03:58.989
Check it out in the video description.

00:03:58.989 --> 00:04:02.900
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

